                            h3.mb-0 Technical Lead
                            .subheading.mb-3 Oracle Cloud Infrastructure, Seattle
                            p.mb-3 Technical lead building distributed control planes for AI infrastructure and cloud storage. Leads ambiguous, cross-organizational programs from architecture through production operations.
                            p.font-italic.mb-2 Repair Management
                            ul
                                li Architected an end-to-end GPU fabric link-repair workflow spanning 7+ backend systems and organizations, enabling targeted replacement and validation of 9,191 interfaces across 8,449 compute trays in seven months, saving approximately 1,000+ hours of manual verification and eliminating the risk of manual-process errors.
                                li Led a four-engineer team and mentored three engineers to deliver event-driven switch-link repair orchestration, replacing poll-based coordination with Kafka events and enforcing repair deduplication across both endpoints of a physical link.
                                li Established a weekly operational-review program and global on-call handoffs that reduced the unresolved high-severity backlog by 80&ndash;82% (66 to 12) and aged high-severity tickets by approximately 94% (64 to 4).
                                li Increased resolution throughput from approximately 1.5 to 6.2 tickets per day &mdash; roughly 4x &mdash; shifting the organization from accumulating backlog to resolving more issues than it received.
                                li Reduced p90 resolution time by approximately 64%, from 78.8 to 28.1 days, and reduced average resolution time by approximately 42%, from 18.4 to 10.7 days.
                                li Designed idempotent, lease-based batch automation that processed 76,946 repairs across 2,025 racks, safely coordinating inventory, custody, customer visibility, ticketing, and post-repair validation.
                                li Led two engineers in onboarding 30 datasets to a standardized analytics platform, resulting in 34 production pipelines that exported more than 15.5 million repair-domain records for leadership forecasting and operational planning.
                                li Authored the architecture for a multi-stage rack-frame repair workflow spanning five stakeholder groups, eight major state transitions, five APIs, 36 transferred components, and 27 validated devices.

                            .case-study.mt-4.ml-3.p-3.bg-light.border-left.border-primary(style='border-left-width: 4px !important;')
                                a.case-study-toggle.d-flex.align-items-center.justify-content-between.collapsed(data-toggle='collapse', href='#cs-gb200-link-triage', role='button', aria-expanded='false', aria-controls='cs-gb200-link-triage')
                                    span.font-italic GB200 GPU-to-Leaf Triage Framework
                                    i.fas.fa-chevron-down.case-study-toggle-icon
                                #cs-gb200-link-triage.collapse
                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Situation
                                        p.mb-2 With rapid GB200 fleet expansion and increasing rack deployment, GPU-to-leaf connectivity failures became a high-impact operational risk. Customers did not have a precise mechanism to report GPU-to-leaf link failures. Issues were often surfaced broadly as GB200 server failures, even when the underlying failure domain was a degraded or failed GPU &harr; BLEAF/FLEAF path.
                                        p.mb-2 Without early link-level identification, teams could not consistently apply the appropriate link-specific SOPs. This often resulted in broader Compute Tray Replacement before the GPU-to-leaf failure was isolated, increasing hardware handling and repair costs and contributing to customer-credit exposure for unavailable rack capacity.
                                        p.mb-0 TPMs also had to manually coordinate across CHS/DCO, hardware teams, software teams, and repair owners to identify the impacted rack, server, GPU, switch, cable path, and remediation plan. This increased manual operational effort, slowed triage, and created ambiguity around repair ownership.

                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Action
                                        ul.mb-0
                                            li Architected the end-to-end #[code GB200_LINK_REPAIR] workflow for both customer-facing and internal repair orders.
                                            li Established cross-team alignment across CHS/on-ground operations, CPV Tier 1, Repair Platform, Warminator, and TPM stakeholders, defining the triage model, ownership boundaries, required software validations, and hardware remediation actions.
                                            li Designed and developed the CHS repair UI to perform targeted component replacement and the TPM interface to track repair status throughout the workflow.
                                            li Developed Warminator validation probes to validate repaired links before returning the Compute Tray to the customer.
                                            li Enabled CHS to perform targeted GPU-to-leaf repairs by replacing only the affected interface or link-path component &mdash; including transceivers, cables, shuffles, and related components &mdash; instead of defaulting to Compute Tray replacement.
                                            li Added an option to RMA individual link components, further reducing the need for broader hardware replacement.

                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Complexity
                                        p.mb-2 Required coordination across multiple engineering and operational organizations, each with different systems, workflows, ownership boundaries, and operational requirements. Integrated the GB200 Link Repair workflow across 7+ backend systems/organizations, requiring alignment on APIs, repair states, hardware actions, validation, and ownership.
                                        p.mb-0 Drove cross-team agreement on the triage and remediation model, ensuring that software diagnostics, physical repair actions, and repair-platform state remained consistent throughout the lifecycle. Partnered with leadership to define usage patterns, API surfaces, link-repair specifications, and the issue-reporting process.

                                    .case-study-block.mb-0
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Result
                                        ul.mb-0
                                            li Eliminated significant manual handoffs between customers, TPMs, and multiple repair teams, reducing a process that could take weeks to resolve a single link-repair issue.
                                            li Accelerated identification and resolution of GPU-to-leaf connectivity failures, enabling targeted component repair rather than broader Compute Tray replacement.
                                            li Reduced unnecessary hardware handling and repair scope and helped lower customer-credit exposure associated with unavailable rack capacity.
                                            li Scaled the workflow to repair 9,191 distinct interfaces across 8,449 compute trays over 7+ months, spanning 7+ backend systems/organizations.
                            p.font-italic.mb-2.mt-4 Object Storage
                            ul
                                li Designed and productionized automated database-index rebuilding for high-scale metadata shards, eliminating approximately 90 engineer-hours of manual work per month in one region, reclaiming storage, preventing customer-facing listing failures, and reclaiming over 1,000+ PB of data across all regions over 3 years.
                                li Led a three-engineer, cross-region program to decompose the metadata monolith and migrate approximately 30 modules/components from Java 8 to Java 17, covering build systems, runtime images, cryptographic compliance, observability, testing, and staged rollout.
                                li Improved Java 17 workload performance by 5.9% for mixed traffic and 15.2% for GET-heavy traffic, while improving p99.95 latency from 70ms to 35.7ms and reducing publisher-run latency by approximately 14&ndash;15%.
                                li Designed load-aware shard rebalancing for a 99.95% availability target, replacing hour-plus manual bucket migrations with automated identification and movement of high-load buckets.
                                li Designed and hardened tenant-, namespace-, and API-level overload protection for a Tier-1 metadata request path, adding controlled throttling, rollout gates, validation dashboards, alarms, and rollback mechanisms.

                            .case-study.mt-4.ml-3.p-3.bg-light.border-left.border-primary(style='border-left-width: 4px !important;')
                                a.case-study-toggle.d-flex.align-items-center.justify-content-between.collapsed(data-toggle='collapse', href='#cs-load-heuristic-rebalancing', role='button', aria-expanded='false', aria-controls='cs-load-heuristic-rebalancing')
                                    span.font-italic Load Heuristic Rebalancing
                                    i.fas.fa-chevron-down.case-study-toggle-icon
                                #cs-load-heuristic-rebalancing.collapse
                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Situation
                                        p.mb-0 ObjectMDS (Metadata Services) was experiencing latency SLO failures driven by uneven bucket load distribution across shards. The existing Metadata Rebalancer primarily supported space-based rebalancing and could not identify buckets that were driving high request load or shard latency. This created a need for a load-heuristic rebalancing path that could proactively identify high-load buckets and move/reshard them from hot shards to lower-load shards before they contributed to degraded customer experience or tenant unavailability.

                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Action
                                        ul.mb-0
                                            li Demonstrated customer focus by targeting ListObjects and write-path latency, which directly contributes to Object Storage SLO performance.
                                            li Designed a Regional Metrics Aggregation architecture to generate region-level bucket load statistics from ObjectMDS logs across hosts.
                                            li Defined the V1 load-based resharding algorithm, using ListObjects request rates and high/low shard latency signals to identify candidate buckets and target shards.
                                            li Integrated load-based decisioning with the existing space-based Metadata Rebalancer safety checks, avoiding a separate migration path and preserving existing operational safeguards.
                                            li Designed the solution for future extensibility, allowing additional metrics such as slow writes, database latency, response codes, and operation-specific filters to be incorporated into load-based decisions.

                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Complexity
                                        p.mb-2 Drove the design of a business-critical operational subsystem affecting Object Storage SLOs and overall MDS fleet health. Required cross-system reasoning across ObjectMDS, ThrottlerService/TopK metrics, T2, MetadataRebalancer, bucket migration/resharding, CDB/PDB shard utilization, and existing space-based rebalancing.
                                        p.mb-2 Accounted for T2 cardinality and query limits, Lumberjack ingestion lag, per-shard bucket attribution, and the need to avoid interference with active bucket migrations. Designed coordination between load-based and space-based rebalancing so that load-driven migrations would not move traffic onto shards that were already poor candidates for other rebalancing decisions.
                                        p.mb-0 Evaluated alternative approaches and selected an extensible MVP that reused existing safety checks, minimizing operational blast radius while enabling a new class of rebalancing decisions.

                                    .case-study-block.mb-0
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Result
                                        ul.mb-0
                                            li Object Storage carries a 99.95% customer-facing success-rate SLO, with latency SLO misses counted as failures; source data showed recurring misses, including RenameObject frequently exceeding the 0.05% failure threshold and DeleteObject reaching 0.078%.
                                            li Prior manual bucket rebalancing required 1+ hour per bucket, with at least one engineer per week dedicated to high-priority manual rebalancing.
                                            li V1 focused on ListObjects load, using top list-request buckets and high/low list-latency shard signals, while leveraging existing resharding safety checks.
                                            li The Regional Metrics Aggregator design supported read, write, and list operation classes with per-minute host-level aggregation for regional statistics.

                            .case-study.mt-4.ml-3.p-3.bg-light.border-left.border-primary(style='border-left-width: 4px !important;')
                                a.case-study-toggle.d-flex.align-items-center.justify-content-between.collapsed(data-toggle='collapse', href='#cs-mds-embargo', role='button', aria-expanded='false', aria-controls='cs-mds-embargo')
                                    span.font-italic MDS Embargo / ObjectMDS Throttling
                                    i.fas.fa-chevron-down.case-study-toggle-icon
                                #cs-mds-embargo.collapse
                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Situation
                                        p.mb-0 ObjectMDS shards needed protection from noisy-neighbor traffic and request overload at the tenant, namespace, and API levels. Without appropriate request limits, high request rates or expensive request patterns could increase metadata DB pressure, heap/GC activity, and latency, potentially impacting shard availability. Designed and implemented default embargo rules for GET, PUT, and LIST paths to protect ObjectMDS before elevated traffic could result in customer-facing latency or availability degradation.

                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Action
                                        ul.mb-0
                                            li Implemented MDS Embargo as a default shard-protection mechanism using EmbargoV3 rules for ObjectMDS.
                                            li Created per-namespace default rules for the three core operation groups: read, write, and list.
                                            li Defined and operationalized regional limits of 25K RPS for reads, 3K RPS for writes, and 2K RPS for lists, translating regional limits into per-host/per-shard rates based on the regional ObjectMDS host count.
                                            li Used controlled HTTP 429 throttling to protect metadata DB availability rather than allowing unbounded request admission to overload MDS.
                                            li Drove the CAPA investigation and remediation after duplicate default-rule creation caused an IAD impact. Disabled automatic rule creation, added duplicate-rule detection, introduced safer #[code casper-go] commands, updated CM templates, and redesigned the Rules Manager flow.
                                            li Reworked the re-enable strategy with configuration-gated rollout, validation dashboards, heap-space checks, and rollback controls.
                                            li Extended the design toward stronger noisy-neighbor fairness and dynamic protection, including per-region rate calculation, DNS/ODO-based host-count discovery, polling-based host-count updates, and future concurrency/dynamic throttling mechanisms.

                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Complexity
                                        p.mb-2 Owned a Tier-1 operational safety mechanism on the Object Storage metadata request path, where overly permissive limits could overload MDS while overly aggressive throttling could unnecessarily reject valid customer traffic. Required deep understanding of ObjectMDS request flow, EmbargoV3 rule evaluation, Operator Service Rules Manager, regional host/shard topology, DB connection pools, heap/GC behavior, and CM rollout mechanics.
                                        p.mb-2 Balanced customer protection and operational safety, protecting shard availability while minimizing unnecessary 429 responses for large tenants. Led CAPA-level analysis of duplicate rule creation, heap growth, GC pauses, rule memory consumption, Operator Service restart behavior, and stale CM-template assumptions.
                                        p.mb-0 Coordinated across ObjectMDS, Operator Service, #[code casper-go], EmbargoV3, ODO/DNS host discovery, Grafana, CM templates, frontend rollout, and future WebServer concurrency throttling. Evaluated multiple throttling approaches, including static request-rate embargo, concurrent-request throttling, dynamic throttling, and noisy-neighbor fairness, while identifying gaps from services that access ObjectDB outside ObjectMDS.

                                    .case-study-block.mb-0
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Result
                                        ul.mb-0
                                            li During the IAD CAPA event, 4,537 Object Storage customers experienced intermittent increased latency for approximately 133 minutes across GET, PUT, and LIST operations.
                                            li p99 latency reached 21.24 seconds across the three APIs, while MDS host availability dropped to 98.5% during the event.
                                            li IAD operated with 66 shards and 50 ObjectMDS hosts. The default configuration created 198 rules per host: 66 shards &times; 3 API groups.
                                            li Each wildcard namespace rule consumed approximately 18 MB of heap. Three rules per shard consumed approximately 54 MB, resulting in approximately 3.5 GB across 66 shards, or ~29.1% of a 12 GB heap.
                                            li Duplicate old and new rules increased heap consumption to approximately 7 GB, driving heap utilization from ~66% to 92% and causing GC pauses.
                                            li Re-enable rollout was instrumented with regional CM/change-ticket tracking, pre/post heap validation, and EmbargoV3 dashboards to verify safe deployment.

                            .case-study.mt-4.ml-3.p-3.bg-light.border-left.border-primary(style='border-left-width: 4px !important;')
                                a.case-study-toggle.d-flex.align-items-center.justify-content-between.collapsed(data-toggle='collapse', href='#cs-index-rebuild', role='button', aria-expanded='false', aria-controls='cs-index-rebuild')
                                    span.font-italic Index Rebuild
                                    i.fas.fa-chevron-down.case-study-toggle-icon
                                #cs-index-rebuild.collapse
                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Situation
                                        p.mb-0 Frequent customer PUTs, bucket migrations, and deletes cause fragmentation within database shards, making indexes increasingly inefficient over time. This resulted in index bloat, tablespace pressure, increased disk utilization, and customer-impacting List latency/failures.

                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Action
                                        ul.mb-0
                                            li Identified and proposed an automated index rebuild solution to reclaim fragmented index space and return recovered capacity for Object Storage usage.
                                            li Independently owned the product lifecycle, from architecture and design through implementation, testing, rollout, monitoring, and production support.
                                            li Developed a feature-based priority-queue algorithm for partition selection, optimizing rebuild ordering based on expected space reclamation.
                                            li Added listing-failure-aware prioritization to prioritize partitions associated with customer-impacting failures.
                                            li Improved temporary-space and buffer-factor calculations in collaboration with DbPerf to make rebuilds safer and more predictable.
                                            li Added database-version gates and de-mirrored shard support, along with partition-level dashboards, metrics, alarms, rollout documentation, testing, and production bug fixes.

                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Complexity
                                        p.mb-2 Required deep ownership across Object Metadata DB internals, OLM listing paths, Oracle index-rebuild behavior, temporary-space constraints, DbPerf/IOD dependencies, T2 metrics, Grafana dashboards, Metadata Rebalancer lifecycle, regional rollout, alerting, and operator workflows.
                                        p.mb-0 The solution also required balancing space reclamation, rebuild safety, temporary storage requirements, customer-facing listing failures, and continuous production workloads when determining which partitions to rebuild and in what order.

                                    .case-study-block.mb-0
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Result
                                        ul.mb-0
                                            li The previous manual process required approximately 2 hours per index rebuild. IAD alone required 45 manual partition rebuilds in 30 days, representing approximately 90 hours of developer/on-call effort.
                                            li Automated and continuously optimized rebuild selection to prioritize partitions based on space reclamation potential and customer impact.
                                            li Example: In GRU, partition SYS_P2143 was reduced from 87.1 GB to 190 KB, with temporary space requirements reduced to approximately 200 KB.
                                            li The system supports high-scale indexes such as Bucket ID and Object Name, including an observed ~1.06 TB index on a 5.87 TB table.
                                            li Over the past two years, the automation has recovered more than ~1,000 PB of cumulative space across OC1 regions.

                            .case-study.mt-4.ml-3.p-3.bg-light.border-left.border-primary(style='border-left-width: 4px !important;')
                                a.case-study-toggle.d-flex.align-items-center.justify-content-between.collapsed(data-toggle='collapse', href='#cs-archive-tier-sunset', role='button', aria-expanded='false', aria-controls='cs-archive-tier-sunset')
                                    span.font-italic Archive Tier Sunset
                                    i.fas.fa-chevron-down.case-study-toggle-icon
                                #cs-archive-tier-sunset.collapse
                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Situation
                                        p.mb-0 OCI Object Storage was deprecating Archive Tier to simplify the storage model from three tiers to two &mdash; Standard and Infrequent Access &mdash; while maintaining customer experience, storage semantics, and billing correctness. The migration required moving Archive-tier buckets to Standard and Archive-tier objects to Infrequent Access, preventing new Archive usage, and subsequently removing Archive Tier from storage workflows and customer-facing interfaces.

                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Action
                                        p.mb-2 Led the design and implementation strategy for the Archive Tier sunset across the Object Storage stack, including:
                                        ul.mb-2
                                            li Bucket creation and storage-tier behavior
                                            li Object upload and storage-tier updates
                                            li Object Lifecycle Management (OLM) policy evaluation and transitions
                                            li Auto-Tiering behavior and customer cost optimization
                                            li Metadata API behavior and storage-tier representation
                                            li Metering, billing, retention, restore, GET/LIST, and PUT/DELETE semantics
                                            li REST API, CLI, and Console behavior
                                            li Storage capacity metrics integration with Horizon for capacity forecasting and planning
                                        p.mb-2 The Archive Tier sunset was executed through two parallel workstreams:
                                        p.mb-2
                                            strong Phase 1:
                                            |  Transparently expose existing Archive buckets as Standard and Archive objects as Infrequent Access, block creation of new Archive resources, update OLM transitions, remove Archive from storage-tier enums and API responses, and preserve backward-compatible customer behavior during the transition.
                                        p.mb-0
                                            strong Phase 2:
                                            |  Migrate all Archive-tier objects to Infrequent Access, complete the required metadata migration/backfill, and fully remove Archive-tier object support from storage workflows and customer-facing surfaces.

                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Complexity
                                        p.mb-0 Drove a cross-surface storage-tier deprecation spanning public APIs, CLI/Console UX, OLM policy semantics, metadata consistency, metering and retention behavior, MDS backfill, and capacity analytics. The design required careful compatibility decisions because Archive Tier existed at both the bucket and object level, with customer-visible implications across billing, restore behavior, retention, object operations, and lifecycle policies. The solution covered 3 customer interaction surfaces &mdash; Console, CLI, and REST APIs &mdash; and 3 object-tier entry points &mdash; upload, explicit tier modification, and OLM policy transitions &mdash; while separating the MVP customer-facing translation layer from the subsequent metadata backfill.

                                    .case-study-block.mb-0
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Result
                                        ul.mb-0
                                            li Simplified OCI Object Storage from a three-tier to a two-tier storage model.
                                            li Impacted 737 paid Archive-tier customers.
                                            li Addressed an Archive footprint of approximately 419 PB.
                                            li Reduced long-term engineering, operational, and support complexity associated with maintaining a dedicated Archive tier.
                                            li Integrated storage capacity metrics with Horizon, improving capacity forecasting and planning.

                            .case-study.mt-4.ml-3.p-3.bg-light.border-left.border-primary(style='border-left-width: 4px !important;')
                                a.case-study-toggle.d-flex.align-items-center.justify-content-between.collapsed(data-toggle='collapse', href='#cs-monolith-decomposition', role='button', aria-expanded='false', aria-controls='cs-monolith-decomposition')
                                    span.font-italic Monolith Decomposition / JDK Upgrade
                                    i.fas.fa-chevron-down.case-study-toggle-icon
                                #cs-monolith-decomposition.collapse
                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Situation
                                        p.mb-0 Metadata Services (MDS) was part of the Object Storage monolith (Frontend / MDS / Storage / OLM / Auto-Tiering / Garbage Collector), with multiple components intertwined and running on Java 8, creating long build cycles, shared dependency ownership, and risk to OCI-wide JDK 17 compliance. Led the MDS repo split and Java 17 migration strategy across critical Object Storage metadata services while maintaining service reliability and rollout safety.

                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Action
                                        p.mb-2 Led a team of five engineers and coordinated with US and IDC subject-matter experts to execute the Java 17 migration across MDS services, including:
                                        ul.mb-0
                                            li Drove monolith decomposition by splitting MDS from shared Object Storage code into MDS- and component-owned repositories, stabilizing builds, removing common coupling, and enabling independent service ownership.
                                            li Sequenced the repo split before the Java 17 migration to reduce dependency blast radius and avoid forcing unrelated Object Storage components through MDS-specific upgrades.
                                            li Defined the Java 8 / Java 17 compatibility strategy using separate build artifacts and runtime selection, enabling a controlled rollout instead of a flag-day runtime migration.
                                            li Implemented Java 17 prerequisites, including Prism metrics and Jipher adoption, to satisfy OCI cryptography/FIPS compliance requirements and expose JCE provider usage.
                                            li Upgraded build, test, and runtime dependencies, including the Maven Compiler Plugin, Mockito, Guice, SpotBugs, Lombok, and service-specific libraries.
                                            li Updated OCI Build configuration, Docker runtime images, startup scripts, server-check validation, Surefire/Failsafe JVM args, and module-level build steps for Java 17.
                                            li Authored upgrade guidance and a Casper-wide dependency inventory so other Object Storage teams could follow a consistent migration path.

                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Complexity
                                        p.mb-0 Drove a cross-cutting migration across source layout, Maven reactor dependencies, OCI Build steps, runtime images, JVM flags, deployment validation, and service-level performance qualification. Required handling Java 17 module-boundary issues such as #[code --add-opens] / #[code --add-exports], reflection access, internal JDK APIs, dependency convergence, and runtime-only failures after a successful compile. Required migrating the security provider from BC-FIPS to Jipher, with Prism observability, before moving critical services to Java 17. Touched core MDS services and surrounding components, including Object MDS, Tenant MDS, Operator MDS, Work Request MDS, Schema Service, MDS Canary, Storage Metering, RQS, Garbage Collector, Metadata Rebalancer, CMSP, and Snapshot Publisher &mdash; requiring ownership across architecture sequencing, cross-org coordination, operational excellence, and data-driven rollout qualification.

                                    .case-study-block.mb-0
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Result
                                        ul.mb-0
                                            li Completed repo split phases for independent MDS ownership, casper-common dependency removal, and build/test optimization.
                                            li Built a Casper dependency inventory covering approximately 50 Object Storage components across MDS, FE, Storage, SDP, Async, and Webserver paths, and a Java 17 plan tracking roughly 30 MDS modules/components through upgrade and rollout readiness.
                                            li Object MDS Java 17 performance qualification showed no regression across mixed GET/PUT/LIST/DELETE traffic; mixed-workload throughput improved from 5,567 to 5,897 ops/sec, while GC pause time dropped from 2.63% to 0.00063%.
                                            li 90% GET / 10% PUT Object MDS testing improved from 9,022 to 10,396 ops/sec, with p99.95 latency improving from 70ms to 35.7ms.
                                            li Metadata Publisher Java 17 qualification improved CMSP run latency by roughly 14&ndash;15% across ~150M and ~280M object test sets.
                        .flex-shrink-0
                            span.text-primary February 2021 - Present

                    .d-flex.flex-column.flex-md-row.justify-content-between.mb-5
                        .flex-grow-1
                            h3.mb-0 Software Developer Intern
                            .subheading.mb-3 Amazon Web Services, Seattle &mdash; AWS EC2 Security
                            ul
                                li Designed an end-to-end architecture to enhance customer security by removing outdated data stored in S3 buckets.
                                li Provided an API and CLI option for clients to set the number of days they want data to be stored.

                            .case-study.mt-4.ml-3.p-3.bg-light.border-left.border-primary(style='border-left-width: 4px !important;')
                                a.case-study-toggle.d-flex.align-items-center.justify-content-between.collapsed(data-toggle='collapse', href='#cs-seatbelt-sweeper', role='button', aria-expanded='false', aria-controls='cs-seatbelt-sweeper')
                                    span.font-italic Seatbelt Execution Output Sweeper
                                    i.fas.fa-chevron-down.case-study-toggle-icon
                                #cs-seatbelt-sweeper.collapse
                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Situation
                                        p.mb-0 Seatbelt is a managed service for executing scripts at scale across hosts, providing safety guardrails, velocity control, monitoring, and other execution controls. When customers execute scripts through Seatbelt, script output and error logs are stored in S3; these logs can contain sensitive or critical data from individual hosts and therefore need to be automatically deleted after an appropriate retention period. The goal of this project was to design and implement an automated mechanism to expire and delete Seatbelt execution logs from S3, reducing data-security and compliance risk while giving customers control over log retention.

                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Action
                                        p.mb-2 Drove the project across four workstreams:
                                        ul.mb-0
                                            li
                                                strong Defined log-retention requirements:
                                                |  Engaged with recent and frequent customers and EC2 Security Engineers to determine appropriate minimum, maximum, and default values for the Log Expiration Days configuration.
                                            li
                                                strong Customer-facing configuration:
                                                |  Implemented API and CLI changes to allow customers to configure and update Log Expiration Days.
                                            li
                                                strong Automated log cleanup:
                                                |  Implemented multithreaded DJS jobs to identify and delete expired execution logs from S3 and maintain the corresponding Log Deletion Time.
                                            li
                                                strong Production execution:
                                                |  Built the synthesis DJS job using LPT to orchestrate the cleanup workflow at scale.

                                    .case-study-block
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Complexity
                                        p.mb-2 A key design challenge was performing large-scale log cleanup without increasing database load or affecting processes already running on the same fleet.
                                        p.mb-2 During design review, I identified that introducing a new indexed Log Expiration Status field could increase database load and negatively impact existing workloads. Instead, I reused an existing indexed status attribute to represent the required state, avoiding the need for an additional database index and reducing the potential impact on fleet performance.
                                        p.mb-0 I also initially proposed using a separate DJS job as a backup worker for records whose S3 deletion failed. Based on design feedback, I redesigned the workflow to reuse the same DJS job &mdash; first processing newly expired logs, then retrying records that had remained undeleted for more than a day. This eliminated a separate worker, reducing resource and memory consumption while keeping the cleanup and retry logic within a single workflow.

                                    .case-study-block.mb-0
                                        strong.text-primary.case-study-label Result
                                        p.mb-2 The solution applies to all Seatbelt users and automatically manages expiration for approximately 1,000 script executions per day, covering ~40 GB of execution output daily and 1,200+ GB per month across 30K+ execution logs.
                                        p.mb-0 By automatically expiring and deleting execution output from S3, the solution reduces the exposure window for potentially sensitive host data, lowers S3 storage cost, mitigates data-security and compliance risk, and eliminates the need for manual log cleanup.
                        .flex-shrink-0
                            span.text-primary June 2020 - August 2020

